{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat","id":1030606,"title":"Unarmed vs. Armed Combat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-12T08:48:46-08:00","id":1030606,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Unarmed vs. Armed Combat","content":"So reading through the Unarmed Combat section, brawlers have a fairly significant advantage over Weaponry based characters. <br /> <br /> Disarms:<br /> Strength+Brawl vs. Strength+Athletics is catering directly to the brawler. An amazing highly trained swordsman with Fighting Finess may have a snowflakes chance in hell to avoid being disarmed. Nevermind that avoiding being disarmed is part of learning how to fight with a weapon.<br /> <br /> Grappling: <br /> Grappling is similarly tilted towards the unarmed fighter. They suffer no penalty to try and grapple someone with a weapon. Go after someone with a knife? No problem. Get around a rapier? No problem. You only subtract your opponent's defense to initiate a grapple and then the weaponry based fighter is in your balliwick and can't try to stab or shoot you until they win a Strength+Brawl vs. Strength+Brawl roll. <br /> <br /> This just seems like a rather big broken spot in the combat system. Am I missing something? How do you have a weaponry or gun fighter that isn't going to get grappled like a chump because they're not heavily invested in strength and brawl?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10769-llegume","name":"LLegume","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T09:08:12-08:00","id":1030613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat#post1030606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This just seems like a rather big broken spot in the combat system. Am I missing something?</div> </div> </div> </div>Guns have range and ignore defense. You're not going to grapple someone before dying if they have multiple (or even one, in some cases) rounds of free lethal to throw at you.<br /> <br /> Melee weapons require an order of magnitude less investment to do respectable amounts of lethal damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T09:30:13-08:00","id":1030621,"title":"","content":"Guns are definitely better than both brawl and weapons but I do agree it seems silly to be able to grapple someone holding a weapon without some form of penalty or drawback. Potential house rules could be to add weapon size to defense (or on Dodge after Defense is doubled) when avoiding a grappling attempt and/or deal weapon damage (or 1B if the weapon has 0 weapon mod) to the would-be grappler on failed grappling attempts. In grappling, however, I think it's quite realistic to have to use Brawl in order to cause damage with your weapon since you can't use any weaponry maneuvers or aim properly when someone is within your weapon range.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T10:31:46-08:00","id":1030655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat#post1030606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This just seems like a rather big broken spot in the combat system. Am I missing something?</div> </div> </div> </div>Primarily thinking it's broken. A brawler is good at brawling. But brawling only deals Bashing damage without special Merits, meaning he has to take twice as long as a weapon-user to incapacitate someone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How do you have a weaponry or gun fighter that isn't going to get grappled like a chump because they're not heavily invested in strength and brawl? </div> </div> </div>A gun fighter would presumably take the advantage of a gun and shoot from a distance while the target has no Defense.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11887_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11887\" data-vbnamecard=\"11887\">Tessie</a> If someone manages to control their weapon during the grapple, they can use it to inflict Lethal damage. There's also a variety of weapons that offer benefits to use in a Grapple, but it's the kind of thing that doesn't seem like it should be a 'given' without some special training.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T11:22:22-08:00","id":1030684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030655#post1030655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Primarily thinking it's broken. A brawler is good at brawling. But brawling only deals Bashing damage without special Merits, meaning he has to take twice as long as a weapon-user to incapacitate someone.</div> </div> </div> </div>A brawler is good at brawling, and that's fine. But a swordsman should be able to be good at swordsmanship. Disarms and grappling the swordsman bypass all weaponry skill entirely and the amazing brawler isn't penalized for going after someone empty handed when their enemy is lethally armed. I like Tessie's proposal to add weaponry size to the defense at the very least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10769-llegume","name":"LLegume","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T11:26:39-08:00","id":1030687,"title":"","content":"During a grapple, yes. I was talking about trying to initiate a grapple. IRL if you try to jump someone you really, really don't want them to hold a sharp weapon. Even if they don't intend to retaliate you're going to risk getting sliced. The system does not reflect that risk or the fact that it's harder to get close to someone holding a weapon even if it isn't sharp.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T11:49:22-08:00","id":1030702,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030684#post1030684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A brawler is good at brawling, and that's fine. But a swordsman should be able to be good at swordsmanship.</div> </div> </div> </div>They are. Brawlers having an an advantage at grappling doesn't take away from that. If a swordsman wants to be good at grappling there's easy ways to do that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Disarms and grappling the swordsman bypass all weaponry skill entirely and the amazing brawler isn't penalized for going after someone empty handed when their enemy is lethally armed. </div> </div> </div>They're contested rolls and do no damage on their own, unless you get an Exceptional Success in Grapple. If you're in a fight and fail either of these, you've effectively wasted a turn against an opponent that deals Lethal damage. Aside from that, there are abilities that allow for the use of different Attributes and effects. If for some reason your swordsman doesn't have Strength he can use Fighting Finesse to use Dexterity instead, and there's more than a few ways to punish characters for just getting close to you, such as the Armed Defense and Heavy Weapons styles, the Retain Weapon and Armed Restraint Merits.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11887_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11887\" data-vbnamecard=\"11887\">Tessie</a> the system does reflect that risk, as the person who controls the weapon can then use that weapon in the grapple.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T12:11:01-08:00","id":1030711,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat#post1030606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So reading through the Unarmed Combat section, brawlers have a fairly significant advantage over Weaponry based characters.<br /> <br /> Disarms:<br /> Strength+Brawl vs. Strength+Athletics is catering directly to the brawler. An amazing highly trained swordsman with Fighting Finess may have a snowflakes chance in hell to avoid being disarmed. Nevermind that avoiding being disarmed is part of learning how to fight with a weapon.<br /> <br /> Grappling:<br /> Grappling is similarly tilted towards the unarmed fighter. They suffer no penalty to try and grapple someone with a weapon. Go after someone with a knife? No problem. Get around a rapier? No problem. You only subtract your opponent's defense to initiate a grapple and then the weaponry based fighter is in your balliwick and can't try to stab or shoot you until they win a Strength+Brawl vs. Strength+Brawl roll.<br /> <br /> This just seems like a rather big broken spot in the combat system. Am I missing something? How do you have a weaponry or gun fighter that isn't going to get grappled like a chump because they're not heavily invested in strength and brawl?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Weapons inflict Beaten Down easily.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:22:12-08:00","id":1030749,"title":"","content":"I presume the weaponry or gun fighter kills the brawler while the brawler is trying to grapple so they don't get killed, necessary because their ability to kill is not good. That would be realistic. A judo master needs to disarm a fencer with sword before getting stabbed while the fencer doesn't really need to worry about defending at all. I think it works out in the wash.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1526,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","user":"Diggs","date":". 01-12-2017, 04:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:30:23-08:00","id":1030751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030655#post1030655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A brawler is good at brawling. But brawling only deals Bashing damage without special Merits<br /> <br /> (...)<br /> <br /> If someone manages to control their weapon during the grapple, they can use it to inflict Lethal damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>That raises an interesting point - a brawl specialist could actually make excellent use of their own weapon, even without any training in weaponry, simply by using it to insure there's a weapon involved in the grapple to use the Control Weapon maneuver on. Also, a brawler can always just pick up a set of brass knuckles if they want to do lethal damage easily.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T13:41:29-08:00","id":1030759,"title":"","content":"You initiate a grapple. If you get less than 5 successes, you win the right to actually do something. While you do that, I stab you. If I succeed you have to spend 1 WP per turn to keep fighting, assuming Beaten Down is in play. Works fine. <br /> <br /> I do agree Disarm isn't well-written -- it's superior to grappling. I would make it a variation of Grappling/Control Weapon (where you fling it away) while unarmed, or an Exceptional Success option while armed, because disarming someone is actually pretty tough. I would also clarify that even if you successfully initiate a grapple, you don't hinder an opponent's attacks until the following turn. So you can get stabbed.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T14:01:07-08:00","id":1030776,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030751#post1030751\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That raises an interesting point - a brawl specialist could actually make excellent use of their own weapon, even without any training in weaponry, simply by using it to insure there's a weapon involved in the grapple to use the Control Weapon maneuver on. Also, a brawler can always just pick up a set of brass knuckles if they want to do lethal damage easily.</div> </div> </div> </div>While using a weapon might be a good idea, the specific 'Damage' maneuver in a grapple has you add the weapon modifier to successes. Brass knuckles do L damage, but have a modifier of 0. Though using them or some other fist-weapon is a good idea for someone who's specialized with their fists. Just like having Retain Weapon or other skills are good for someone specialized with weapons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T14:07:26-08:00","id":1030780,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030776#post1030776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While using a weapon might be a good idea, the specific 'Damage' maneuver in a grapple has you add the weapon modifier to successes. Brass knuckles do L damage, but have a modifier of 0. Though using them or some other fist-weapon is a good idea for someone who's specialized with their fists. Just like having Retain Weapon or other skills are good for someone specialized with weapons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. I don't think brawl is overpowered by any means (and I wouldn't really be bothered if it was, CofD isn't a game where mathematical parity of damage output is important), just pointing out a cool interaction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T14:17:04-08:00","id":1030789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030759#post1030759\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You initiate a grapple. If you get less than 5 successes, you win the right to actually do something. While you do that, I stab you. If I succeed you have to spend 1 WP per turn to keep fighting, assuming Beaten Down is in play. Works fine.<br /> <br /> I do agree Disarm isn't well-written -- it's superior to grappling. I would make it a variation of Grappling/Control Weapon (where you fling it away) while unarmed, or an Exceptional Success option while armed, because disarming someone is actually pretty tough. I would also clarify that even if you successfully initiate a grapple, you don't hinder an opponent's attacks until the following turn. So you can get stabbed.</div> </div> </div> </div>You initiate a grapple. You get less than 5 successes but you're still grappling and your opponent with a knife loses their turn because they're grappled and can't make a normal attack. When you hit the subsequent turn you're into Strengh+Brawl contested rolls and the weaponry user is hosed because their weapon was useless for defending themselves. <br /> <br /> Also, something like Spear and Bayonet 2 (Keep at Bay) is <i>completely</i> useless at actually keeping a grappler at bay because it doesn't <i>matter</i> if the grappler loses his Defense as a penalty for coming at you. His Defense never actually comes into play once a grapple is initiated. <br /> <br /> I'm not adverse to a grappler having the advantage once they're in the grapple but it should not be so easy and so safe to grapple someone who's skilled in wielding a lethal weapon. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10769-llegume","name":"LLegume","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T14:41:49-08:00","id":1030804,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030789#post1030789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You initiate a grapple. You get less than 5 successes but you're still grappling and your opponent with a knife loses their turn because they're grappled and can't make a normal attack. When you hit the subsequent turn you're into Strengh+Brawl contested rolls and the weaponry user is hosed because their weapon was useless for defending themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>It doesn't convey any special advantage on its own, as noted before there are things you can have to make the weapon useful. But a weaponry user is likely to have Strength to begin with, as that's a part of using their weapon. And that first roll is against their Defense.<br /> <br /> A poor knifeman against a superior grappler is going to end badly for the knifeman.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, something like Spear and Bayonet 2 (Keep at Bay) is <i>completely</i> useless at actually keeping a grappler at bay because it doesn't <i>matter</i> if the grappler loses his Defense as a penalty for coming at you. His Defense never actually comes into play once a grapple is initiated. </div> </div> </div>As is usual in these depictions, you're describing a 1 on 1 fight, but the game is built with more than one player character in mind. That said, solo games aren't unheard of, and the rule is the same there as in others. If you want to be really good, you have to focus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not adverse to a grappler having the advantage once they're in the grapple but it should not be so easy and so safe to grapple someone who's skilled in wielding a lethal weapon. </div> </div> </div>If they're skilled in combat they'd likely have a high Defense, which would keep the grapple from occurring in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-12-2017, 06:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T15:18:46-08:00","id":1030820,"title":"","content":"It's worth bearing in mind that breaking free of a grapple is a reflexive action, meaning that armed characters have a chance to disentangle <i>and </i>use their weapons if their strength + brawl pool isn't likely to win subsequent rounds. <br /> <br /> Still, it's an exceptionally effective way to lock down an armed target. Defense isn't that great of, well, a defense because grapplers have very little incentive not to spend a willpower and all out attack to initiate if they're confident they can win the subsequent rolls.<br /> <br /> Also, given that we're already talking Hurt Locker merits in this thread, it's hard to imagine a character investing in grappling as a skill and not taking Clinch Strike, the one dot merit with minimal prereqs that allows you to deal damage with the same roll that initiates the grapple.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T16:06:37-08:00","id":1030844,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030820#post1030820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's worth bearing in mind that breaking free of a grapple is a reflexive action, meaning that armed characters have a chance to disentangle <i>and </i>use their weapons if their strength + brawl pool isn't likely to win subsequent rounds. </div> </div> </div> </div>They only get to choose to break free if they're the ones who win the grapple roll in a turn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T16:54:16-08:00","id":1030864,"title":"","content":"Yes, that's why I said they have a<i> chance. </i>My wording could have been clearer, but my point is that since breaking free doesn't use up your action, grappling won't <i>necessarily</i> deny somebody the use of their weaponry skill in a given turn, it just makes it dependent on winning the brawl roll first.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T17:28:23-08:00","id":1030881,"title":"","content":"Thanks. Yes, your wording was confusing. My point was that the weapon user will need a lucky break to be able to consider this, as they need to win the Str + Brawl contested roll before they get to choose what move is used, and the pools are likely to be weighted in favour of the person who specialises in unarmed combat. If the dice happen to go the way of the weapon wielder, their best bet is break free and use their weapon. I agree with you.<br /> <br /> But that said, I'm not supporting the OPs point. Once a fight gets to grapple, the person who specialises in grappling should have the advantage. If the weapon wielder can't keep the grappler at bay and get hits in first, then it makes sense that they get taken to the ground and lose the advantage. Of course that part is tilted towards the unarmed fighter.<br /> <br /> Edit: All this is white room thinking anyway, which is rarely specifically useful to how things go down in actual games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T19:31:25-08:00","id":1030964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030702#post1030702\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> the system does reflect that risk, as the person who controls the weapon can then use that weapon in the grapple.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's after the grapple is initiated (although that risk can just as well be a boon since the experienced grappler is so much more likely to be able to use the weapon). I was still talking about trying to initiate the grapple. The system does not reflect any extra difficulty that should realistically arise the moment the opponent grabs a weapon. I'm all for that an experienced grappler would more than likely dominate their opponent when in a grapple, but it's not very realistic that weapons are completely ignored when you're about to initiate the grapple.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T20:42:28-08:00","id":1030992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LLegume</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030789#post1030789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You initiate a grapple. You get less than 5 successes but you're still grappling and your opponent with a knife loses their turn because they're grappled and can't make a normal attack. </div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't interpret the rule that way. The rule for grabbing an opponent merely says they're involved in a grapple, not that anybody loses their turn to attack normally. The grappling rules clearly say you move to grappling maneuvers, &quot;During each subsequent <u>turn</u>&quot; (emphasis mine) not simply upon the initiation of a grapple. So indeed, I *do* stab you when you grab me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T21:16:43-08:00","id":1031001,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1030992#post1030992\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I wouldn't interpret the rule that way. The rule for grabbing an opponent merely says they're involved in a grapple, not that anybody loses their turn to attack normally. The grappling rules clearly say you move to grappling maneuvers, &quot;During each subsequent <u>turn</u>&quot; (emphasis mine) not simply upon the initiation of a grapple. So indeed, I *do* stab you when you grab me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Chronicles of Darkness uses &quot;turn&quot; to describe what most RPGs call a &quot;round&quot;, and &quot;action&quot; or &quot;initiative&quot; to describe what most RPGs call a &quot;turn&quot;. The GMC rules update is a bit more explicit as to how it works.<br /> <br /> &quot;To grab your opponent, roll Strength + Brawl \u2013 Defense. On a success, both of you are grappling. (...) When grappling, each party makes a contested Strength + Brawl versus Strength + Brawl action on the higher of the two characters\u2019 Initiative. - God Machine Chronicle, page 199.<br /> <br /> So, if the character with the higher initiative is the one who initiates the grapple, the other does effectively lose their action for that turn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 01-13-2017, 12:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T22:45:55-08:00","id":1031015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1031001#post1031001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Chronicles of Darkness uses &quot;turn&quot; to describe what most RPGs call a &quot;round&quot;, and &quot;action&quot; or &quot;initiative&quot; to describe what most RPGs call a &quot;turn&quot;. The GMC rules update is a bit more explicit as to how it works.<br /> <br /> &quot;To grab your opponent, roll Strength + Brawl \u2013 Defense. On a success, both of you are grappling. (...) When grappling, each party makes a contested Strength + Brawl versus Strength + Brawl action on the higher of the two characters\u2019 Initiative. - God Machine Chronicle, page 199.<br /> <br /> So, if the character with the higher initiative is the one who initiates the grapple, the other does effectively lose their action for that turn.</div> </div> </div> </div>The rules for moving to grappling actions specify that they occur on the turn (yes, I know what it means) subsequent to the previous turn, as I quoted from the CofD core. The only exception is the ability to use a grappling move if you score an exceptional success. The function of a grab is to otherwise begin a grapple, but the grapple doesn't actually impose itself on both parties until the turn <i>after</i> the grab.<br /> <br /> I mean I'll tell you right out: Grappling doesn't work right if you immediately establish a grapple upon a grab, and back in 2003, when we were fretting over this rule in its playtest (it carries over from the old rules), grabbing someone did not establish a grapple on the same turn. Game design being what it is this might have mutated over the intervening 13 years and WW rules sets are notoriously subjective (for a reason) but in this case I strongly recommend that, well, you do it the way I say it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T23:43:09-08:00","id":1031027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1031015#post1031015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The rules for moving to grappling actions specify that they occur on the turn (yes, I know what it means) subsequent to the previous turn, as I quoted from the CofD core. The only exception is the ability to use a grappling move if you score an exceptional success. The function of a grab is to otherwise begin a grapple, but the grapple doesn't actually impose itself on both parties until the turn <i>after</i> the grab.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, what it says is just the opposite; the exact wording is &quot;on a success, <i>both characters are grappling</i>.&quot; It also says, in a different paragraph, that on subsequent turns, both grappling characters make the contested Strength + Brawl roll on the higher of the two characters' initiatives.<br /> <br /> If both characters are grappling as soon as the initiating character rolls a success, what allows the other character to take an action despite being in a grapple?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1031015#post1031015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean I'll tell you right out: Grappling doesn't work right if you immediately establish a grapple upon a grab, and back in 2003, when we were fretting over this rule in its playtest (it carries over from the old rules), grabbing someone did not establish a grapple on the same turn.</div> </div> </div> </div>Uhh, yes, it did. World of Darkness, page 157: &quot;Roll Strength + Brawl to achieve a hold. The target's Defense is subtracted from your attack pool, as normal. If you get at least one success, your character has a hold of the target.<br /> If the victim has yet to act in the turn, he may try and break loose at his stage of Initiative. Alternatively, the target may try to turn the tables on his opponent and perform an overpowering grappling maneuver of his own. In either case, roll Strength + Brawl, but the attacker's Strength is subtracted from the dice pool.&quot;<br /> It then goes on to describe the various overpowering maneuvers that can be performed with a success on the Strength + Brawl roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1031015#post1031015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Game design being what it is this might have mutated over the intervening 13 years and WW rules sets are notoriously subjective (for a reason) but in this case I strongly recommend that, well, you do it the way I say it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Great. I'mma keep running it the way I run it and you keep running it the way you run it. I'm just pointing out what the book says. People are free to interpret that by their own best judgment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T23:51:12-08:00","id":1031030,"title":"","content":"The text fully supports Charlaquin's method of running this. (That method is what the text explicitly describes).<br /> I've run it the same way for years, and I can assure you that it does, in fact, 'work right'.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T00:13:20-08:00","id":1031032,"title":"","content":"So, what would you do as a weaponry fighter before the grapple? In my experience of playing these games when it comes to melee if a brawler is involved the brawler typically wins. The only benefit I've ever had as a weaponry fighter is going first, but after that if the brawler isn't down then I'm typically going to lose the combat; there's also the fact that other players will be busy dealing with said opponent's allies typically, and they won't be helping you anytime soon. Of course this may be just because I've had a history of terrible dice rolls, and other people aren't typically always rolling really low.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T00:35:30-08:00","id":1031035,"title":"","content":"It's a bit more complicated than &quot;what would you do?&quot; because not every Weaponry based fighter is built the same way.<br /> <br /> The optimal way to avoid getting grappled is to pick up Merits that allow you to boost your Defense and apply damage to your opponent. Armed Defense 3 + 4 with a decent damage weapon can easily stack the odds in your favor (at least until you run out of WP) since you're going to be insanely hard to hit and you have good odds of doing lethal to in return. It even can resist multiple opponents for a bit (and if Beaten Down is in effect, it's pretty simple to apply the Tilt to all of a group of attackers and then try to wait them out).<br /> <br /> The other primary option is to do what most people that engage in life-or-death violence on a regular basis do: diversify. Anyone expected to be ready to fight in a serious situation is trained in multiple disciplines. Cops don't just use guns. They learn to wield various weapons and how to fight unarmed. Same goes for soldiers. Pretty much every fencing/sword-fighting manual from history clearly shows that training to use a sword also included training in things that would fall under Brawl in the CofD system.<br /> <br /> If your character only bothers to learn how to shoot, or how to swing a blade, or whatever focus, they're vulnerable to tactics designed to shut that down. That's why people that have to fight try to avoid being one-trick ponies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T08:02:53-08:00","id":1031159,"title":"","content":"Avoiding being grappled is pretty important for other types of unarmed styles too, for that matter. All the fancy Martial Arts moves do nothing if you're locked in a grapple and lose the roll. It's less of an issue for mortals, since the things that divide grappling from other styles are largely merits that increase your options after a successful roll, rather than boosts to said roll, meaning there's a greater chance for the characters to have roughly even odds. When you get to supernaturals though there's quite a few ways to get that pool crazy high. <br /> Luckily, the alternate Martial Arts style in Hurt Locker has a great maneuver called Inch Force which can break a grapple before it begins.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T09:29:26-08:00","id":1031206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1030606-unarmed-vs-armed-combat?p=1031159#post1031159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Avoiding being grappled is pretty important for other types of unarmed styles too, for that matter. All the fancy Martial Arts moves do nothing if you're locked in a grapple and lose the roll. It's less of an issue for mortals, since the things that divide grappling from other styles are largely merits that increase your options after a successful roll, rather than boosts to said roll, meaning there's a greater chance for the characters to have roughly even odds. When you get to supernaturals though there's quite a few ways to get that pool crazy high.</div> </div> </div> </div>Supernaturals have a lot more options, though. Werewolves and vampires can increase their Strength with ease and do Lethal in grapples and heal bashing pretty easily. If your opponent is going to ignore your Bashing damage in a grapple and inflict Lethal, you might be better off not grabbing them. One mage NPC in a game I played had his armor on that made it near impossible to damage him in a grapple.<br /> <br /> And while some Martial Arts fighting styles may offer no special advantage in a grapple, the mere fact you have trained in Strength and Brawl gives you a good lead on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T11:48:13-08:00","id":1031274,"title":"","content":"Clearly the answer of how to avoid being grappled is to be a changeling and Cage a Dream your way out of all grapples as a reflexive action. <i>Then</i> stab the guy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10769-llegume","name":"LLegume","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T12:00:27-08:00","id":1031279,"title":"","content":"With the Tactical Shifting Fighting Style, you can basically kill someone grappling you. You have to have 4 to 6 Harmony, or 2-3 or 7-8 if you have a bundle of Essence to spend.<br /> <br /> 'When your character has another grappled, shifting up reflexively causes automatic lethal damage equal to the difference in Size between her two forms.'<br /> <br /> I believe Unchained would just break you free without any help.<br /> <br /> But like I said, supernaturals have a huge advantage in these things. Mortals are flimsy and would need the other fighting styles or merits to really help out. Though the idea of grappling to end an opponent's advantage is hardly unrealistic.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://art.penny-arcade.com/photos/592358342_Nz7fC/0/2100x20000/592358342_Nz7fC-2100x20000.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-13-2017, 03:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T12:06:49-08:00","id":1031281,"title":"","content":"Werewolves are straight-up masters of grappling, between Tactical Shifting, bites that establish grapples while also doing damage in two three forms, form bonuses to Strength, and a lot of incentive to invest at least minimally in Brawl.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T15:13:47-08:00","id":1031346,"title":"","content":"Beasts are also insanely good grapplers. With Cyclopean Strength and Monster from the Deep if you have a couple of points of Satiety to burn it's not at all hard to take down an entire room in one turn by about Lair 3. And you can couple Advanced Double Jointed with Fighting Finesse for a nasty Dex based combo too, if that's your preference.<br /> <br /> Don't forget the handful of Changeling Kiths that add Wyrd to grapple rolls either. Venombites in particular can be nasty with Potent Kith.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-13T15:22:40-08:00","id":1031353,"title":"","content":"There's also plenty of supernaturals that are exceedingly good at shutting grapplers down. But once we get into the supernatural stuff the balance of Brawl vs. Weaponry gets tossed all over the place because of each supernatural's differing themes and how those get expressed in their powers and drawbacks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}